[38;5;231m[49m⏺[39m Good — that's locked in: hostname-based selection, and the module is a general config surface (named keys in a
  settings object), not a single exported URL. I'll design it so adding a second setting later is editing a table, not
  reshaping the interface.

  Next fork, and it's forced by "across the app."

  [1mThe fork:[0m this repo has two runtimes that can't currently share a file. [38;5;153mapp.js[39m is a bare browser script — no module
  system at all, loaded by [38;5;153m<script src>[39m, everything a global. [38;5;153msrc/index.js[39m and [38;5;153msrc/utils.js[39m are CommonJS,
  [38;5;153mrequire[39m/[38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m, Node-only. A settings module has to pick one, and that choice decides whether "across the app"
  includes the [38;5;153msrc/[39m tree or stops at the browser page.

  - [1mBare script + global[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m sets [38;5;153mwindow.APP_SETTINGS[39m; [38;5;153mindex.html[39m loads it before [38;5;153mapp.js[39m. Buys: works right
    now, zero tooling, nothing else changes, still opens fine from [38;5;153mfile://[39m. Costs: [38;5;153msrc/[39m can't consume it (no [38;5;153mwindow[39m, no
    exports), so "across the app" means the browser half only. Also puts config on a mutable global. Cheap to migrate
    away from later — one file, one consumer.
  - [1mES modules everywhere[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m uses [38;5;153mexport[39m; [38;5;153mindex.html[39m becomes [38;5;153m<script type="module">[39m; [38;5;153msrc/[39m converts from
    [38;5;153mrequire[39m to [38;5;153mimport[39m and [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m gets [38;5;153m"type": "module"[39m. Buys: one module system, a settings file both halves
    genuinely share, and the direction the ecosystem already went. Costs: converts two files that have nothing to do
    with your request, and ES modules are blocked over [38;5;153mfile://[39m by CORS — you'd need [38;5;153mnpx serve[39m to open the page in dev,
    which you don't need today. Not easily undone.
  - [1mDual export wrapper[0m — [38;5;153msettings.js[39m detects [38;5;153mmodule.exports[39m vs [38;5;153mwindow[39m and serves both. Buys: both runtimes, no
    conversion of existing files. Costs: boilerplate on every shared module, and a pattern the ecosystem abandoned
    deliberately.

  [1mMy recommendation depends on something I should just ask rather than assume:[0m [38;5;153msrc/[39m today is a hello-world
  ([38;5;153mgreet('world')[39m) with no connection to the login page — no imports between them, separate entry points. If it's
  scaffolding you don't intend to grow, ES modules buys you a conversion you'll never cash in, and I'd take the bare
  script + global. If [38;5;153msrc/[39m is where the real application is actually going to live, ES modules is worth its cost now,
  because doing it after there are ten files is much worse than after three.

